# Using Maven JXR in Java

The cross-referencing API is small. You can generate a cross-reference for whole source trees, or turn a single Java
source file into one HTML page. Every generated page has an anchor per line number.

Add the library to your project:

```xml
<dependency>
  <groupId>org.apache.maven</groupId>
  <artifactId>maven-jxr</artifactId>
  <version>${project.version}</version>
</dependency>
```

The library logs through SLF4J, so add an SLF4J binding if you want to see its debug output.

${esc.h}${esc.h} Transforming Java Packages

[`JXR`](../apidocs/org/apache/maven/jxr/JXR.html) walks one or more source directories, writes one page per source
file and then renders the package and class index pages. It also takes the input and output encodings, the locale,
include and exclude patterns, and the Javadoc directory to link to.

```java
FileManager fileManager = new FileManager();
PackageManager packageManager = new PackageManager(fileManager);
JavaCodeTransform codeTransform = new JavaCodeTransform(packageManager, fileManager);

JXR jxr = new JXR(packageManager, codeTransform);
jxr.setDest(Paths.get("target/jxr"));
jxr.setLocale(Locale.ENGLISH);
jxr.setInputEncoding("UTF-8");
jxr.setOutputEncoding("UTF-8");

jxr.xref(
        Collections.singletonList("src/main/java"),
        "templates/jdk8",
        "My Project Reference",
        "My Project Reference",
        "Copyright 2026 My Company");
```

The second argument of `xref` names the [Velocity](https://velocity.apache.org/) templates for the index pages. A
relative path such as `templates/jdk8` loads the
[templates bundled with the library](https://github.com/apache/maven-jxr/tree/master/maven-jxr/src/main/resources/templates)
from the class path; `templates/jdk4`, `templates/jdk7` and `templates/jdk8` mimic the Javadoc page layout of that
JDK. An absolute path loads your own templates from that directory.

Set the output encoding: the index pages are written with it, and there is no default.

The output for a project with one class, `com.example.app.Greeter`, looks like this:

```
target/jxr
 |- allclasses-frame.html
 |- com/example/app/Greeter.html
 |- com/example/app/package-frame.html
 |- com/example/app/package-summary.html
 |- index.html
 |- overview-frame.html
 |- overview-summary.html
```

The pages refer to a `stylesheet.css` in the output directory, which the library does not write. The Maven JXR
Plugin copies one there; when you call the library yourself, supply your own.

${esc.h}${esc.h} Transforming a Single Java Source File

You can transform a single Java source file with the following:

```java
FileManager fileManager = new FileManager();
PackageManager packageManager = new PackageManager(fileManager);
JavaCodeTransform codeTransform = new JavaCodeTransform(packageManager, fileManager);

codeTransform.transform(
        Paths.get("src/main/java/com/example/app/Greeter.java"),
        Paths.get("target/jxr-single/Greeter.html"),
        Locale.ENGLISH,
        "UTF-8", // input encoding
        "UTF-8", // output encoding
        null, // relative path to the Javadoc, or null for no "View Javadoc" link
        "", // revision
        ""); // bottom text
```
